1. What is RPA?
RPA (Robotic Process Automation) is a technology used to automate repetitive and rule-based tasks using software robots.
RPA robots can perform tasks that humans normally do on a computer, such as:
Entering data
Copying and pasting information
Reading and writing Excel files
Extracting data from websites
Sending emails
Filling online forms
Moving files between folders
Generating reports
RPA helps organizations reduce manual work, improve accuracy, save time, and increase productivity.

2. What is UiPath?
UiPath is an RPA platform used to design, develop, test, and run software automation workflows.
UiPath provides a visual, low-code environment where automation workflows can be created using different activities instead of writing large amounts of code.

3. Types of RPA Robots
There are mainly three common types of RPA robots based on how and when they operate.

3.1 Attended Robots
Attended robots work together with a human user.
They are usually triggered by the user when assistance is required.
Example:
A customer service employee receives a customer request. The employee starts an RPA robot, and the robot automatically retrieves the required customer information from different systems.
Key point: Human + Robot working together.

3.2 Unattended Robots
Unattended robots work without continuous human involvement.
They can be scheduled or triggered automatically and can execute tasks in the background.
Example:
An organization can schedule a robot to process invoices every night and update the required records automatically.
Key point: Robot works independently without continuous human intervention.

3.3 Hybrid Robots
Hybrid automation combines attended and unattended automation.
A human can start or interact with the automation when required, while other parts of the process can run automatically in the background.
Example:
An employee starts a process to verify customer information, and the robot performs the remaining data processing automatically.
Key point: Combination of attended and unattended automation.

UiPath Studio
1. Introduction to UiPath Studio
UiPath Studio is a development environment provided by UiPath for designing, building, testing, and debugging automation workflows.
It provides a visual, low-code environment where automation can be created by adding and configuring different activities.
UiPath Studio can be used to automate tasks involving:
Web applications
Desktop applications
Excel and spreadsheets
Files and folders
Databases
Emails
Data extraction
Repetitive business processes
The automation created in UiPath Studio is called a workflow.

2. Main Components of UiPath Studio
Some important components of UiPath Studio are:
Activities Panel:
Contains different activities that can be dragged and dropped into the workflow.
Examples:
Click
Type Into
Get Text
Assign
If
For Each
Delay
Message Box
Read Range
Write Range

Designer Panel
The Designer Panel is the main workspace where activities are placed and connected to create the automation workflow.

Properties Panel
The Properties Panel is used to configure the properties and settings of the selected activity.

Project Panel
The Project Panel displays the files and folders belonging to the current automation project.

Output Panel
The Output Panel displays logs, messages, warnings, and errors generated during execution.

3. How to Create a New Project in UiPath Studio
The following steps can be used to create a new automation project.

Step 1: Open UiPath Studio
Open UiPath Studio from the Start menu or desktop.

Step 2: Create a New Project
On the UiPath Studio home screen, select:

New Project → Process
A Process project is commonly used to create general-purpose automation workflows.

Step 3: Enter Project Details
Enter the required information:

Name: Give a meaningful name to the project.
Description: Provide a short description of the automation.

Step 4: Create the Project
Click Create.
UiPath Studio creates the project with the required files and opens the project workspace.

Step 5: Open the Main Workflow
The main workflow file is usually named:
Main.xaml
This is where the automation workflow can be designed.

Step 6: Add Activities
Activities can be added by:
Searching for an activity in the Activities Panel
Dragging and dropping the activity into the Designer Panel

Step 7: Configure Activities
Select each activity and configure its properties using the Properties Panel.

Step 8: Save and Run

After completing the workflow:
Save → Run
UiPath executes the workflow and displays execution information in the Output Panel.

Basic UiPath Project Structure

A newly created UiPath project generally contains files and folders such as:
Project Name/
│
├── Main.xaml
├── project.json
└── Other project files/
Main.xaml
Contains the main automation workflow.
project.json
Contains project configuration information, dependencies, and other project settings.




